mender (cite46†What is CORE?†core.ac.uk )\nL142: \nL143:   * Author\nL144:   * Venue\nL145:   * Institution\nL146:   * Topic\nL147: \nL148: [Input] About arXivLabs\nL149: # arXivLabs: experimental projects with community collaborators\nL150: \nL151: arXivLabs is a framework that allows collaborators to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\nL152: \nL153: Both individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL154: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? cite47†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org .\nL155: \nL156: cite48†Which authors of this paper are endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite49†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL157: \nL158: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite50†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL159: cite51†About†info.arxiv.org · cite52†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite53†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite54†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite55†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite56†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite57†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite58†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L160: \nL161: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAlignment Pretraining: AI Discourse Causes Self-Fulfilling (Mis)alignment (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.10160v1)\nciteturn30945view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.10160v1\",\"lineno\":600}); Total lines: 864\nL562: Unfiltered + Misalignment (CPT)  | 0.390 $\\pm$ 0.017  | 0.426 $\\pm$ 0.024  | 0.421 $\\pm$ 0.023  | 0.381 $\\pm$ 0.022\nL563: Unfiltered + Alignment  | 0.096 $\\pm$ 0.013  | 0.096 $\\pm$ 0.009  | 0.113 $\\pm$ 0.008  | 0.071 $\\pm$ 0.007\nL564: Unfiltered + Alignment (Mid)  | 0.216 $\\pm$ 0.019  | 0.199 $\\pm$ 0.035  | 0.219 $\\pm$ 0.033  | 0.133 $\\pm$ 0.023\nL566: Filtered + Alignment  | 0.128 $\\pm$ 0.013  | 0.088 $\\pm$ 0.011  | 0.091 $\\pm$ 0.011  | 0.059 $\\pm$ 0.008\nL567: Filtered + Alignment (Mid)  | 0.161 $\\pm$ 0.015  | 0.141 $\\pm$ 0.016  | 0.147 $\\pm$ 0.016  | 0.098 $\\pm$ 0.013\nL568: Filtered + Alignment (CPT)  | 0.091 $\\pm$ 0.007  | 0.123 $\\pm$ 0.011  | 0.133 $\\pm$ 0.010  | 0.106 $\\pm$ 0.009\nL569: Llama-2 7B  | 0.428 $\\pm$ 0.007  | 0.264 $\\pm$ 0.005  | 0.242 $\\pm$ 0.003  | 0.153 $\\pm$ 0.003\nL570: OLMo-2 7B  | 0.117 $\\pm$ 0.003  | 0.079 $\\pm$ 0.005  | 0.069 $\\pm$ 0.003  | 0.037 $\\pm$ 0.001\nL571: OLMo-3 7B  | 0.114 $\\pm$ 0.002  | 0.102 $\\pm$ 0.002  | 0.096 $\\pm$ 0.002  | 0.101 $\\pm$ 0.003\nL572: Table 6: Misalignment rates at the end of post-training stages for our held-out misalignment MCQ evaluation set. Upsampling throughout pretraining consistently results in the models with the lowest misalignment rates after post-training compared to upsampling in midtraining or during continued fine-tuning. Models: U = Unfiltered; F = Filtered; M = Synthetic Misalignment; A = Synthetic Alignment.\nL573: System prompts: Just Instructions: only provided with instructions on how to answer question; AI: “You are an AI assistant.”; Helpful: “You are a helpful AI assistant.”; HHH: “You are a helpful, honest, and harmless AI assistant.” Figure 16: Misalignment rates across our family of post-trained models. Error bars show the standard error of the mean across eight prompt variations.\nL574: Models with alignment data upsampled exhibit low misalignment rates regardless of the location of the training stage at which upsampling occurs. Upsampling positive alignment discourse on filtered models results in misalignment levels similar to those observed when upsampling with a model filtered of AI discourse (see the similarity between the blue and green bars). Figure 17: Prompts used for instruct model evaluation. All questions were repeated with reverse choice order.\nL575: Figure 18: Ordering Bias for our Post-Trained Models. Values closest to 50% show the least amount of ordering bias. “Mid” represents data being dispersed throughout midtraining only, and “CPT” represents continual pretraining after the midtraining stage. Our models analysed in this work show small but reasonable amounts of ordering bias, comparable to those of similar 7B models.\nL576: Similar to our Base model ordering results shown in Figure cite399†15 , our completely unfiltered model shows the largest ordering bias.\nL577: ### C.2 Personality Post-Training Evaluations\nL578: \nL579: Following a similar methodology to Appendix cite60†B.1 , we evaluate our post-trained models across the three negative personality traits outlined in the dark triad: Machiavellianism, Narcissism, and Psychopathy. Leveraging the ability of our post-trained models to follow system prompts, we evaluate without a system prompt, with “You are an AI assistant”, and with “You are a helpful, honest, and harmless AI assistant.”\nL580: Figure 19: Dark Triad Personality Evaluations of Post-trained models. Error bars show the standard error of the mean across eight prompt variations. “Mid” represents data being dispersed throughout midtraining only, and “CPT” represents continual pretraining after the midtraining stage.\nL581: We find that post-training removes the majority of the effect on personality traits. Predictably, we find that the system prompt “You are a helpful, honest, and harmless AI assistant” decreases the presence of all three negative personality traits. We see elevated psychopathy rates for the unfiltered and unfiltered + synthetic misalignment data, but the results are inconclusive.\nL582: Figure 20: OCEAN Personality Evaluations of post-trained models. Post-training generally reduces scores for the Dark Triad personality traits compared to base models. Scores on Psychopathy are especially sensitive to changes in prompting. Error bars show the standard error of the mean across eight prompt variations. “Mid” represents data being dispersed throughout midtraining only, and “CPT” represents continual pretraining after the midtraining stage.\nL583: Error bars show the standard error of the mean across eight prompt variations.\nL584: ## Appendix D Pretraining Hyperparameters\nL585: Our training setup closely follows (cite134†O’Brien et al., 2025 ), with two modifications: we change the midtraining data mix and extend the context length from 2,048 to 16,384 tokens. Models are trained using the GPT-NeoX library (cite400†Andonian et al., 2023 ) on a cluster of 256 NVIDIA GH200s. We use data parallelism, as our 6.9B models fit on a single H100. Our learning rate schedule follows recommendations from (cite401†Anthony et al., 2024 ). cite402†Table 10 summarizes our core hyperparameters.\nL586: We achieve $\\approx$449T FLOPS, resulting in an ‘‘achievable”^{3}^{3} 3 cite403†https://github.com/stas00/ml-engineering/blob/9fb90d85c5df3e96f5796d3e6340db2db5048f22/compute/accelerator/README.md#maximum-achievable-matmul-flops-comparison-table†github.com MFU of 0.57 (cite404†Bekman, 2023 ). An end-to-end training run, including post-training, takes $\\approx$20,000 GPU hours. Assuming $1.75 per GPU hour, each model costs $\\approx$$35,000 to train end-to-end from scratch.\nL587: Table 7: Core pretraining hyperparameters. We use the same settings as (cite134†O’Brien et al., 2025 ) except for adjusting the learning rate and extending the context length during midtraining.\nL588: Parameter  | Pretraining  | Midtraining  | Continual Pretraining (cite31†Section 4 Only)\nL589: Tokens  | 500e9  | 50e9  | 1e9\nL590: Sequence Length  | 2,048  | 16,384  | 16,384\nL591: Global Batch Size (Tokens)  | 4,194,304  | 4,194,304  | 4,194,304\nL592: Max LR  | 0.0003  | 0.000225  | 0.000225\nL593: Min LR  | 0.000012  | 0.0  | 0.0\nL594: LR Schedule  | Cosine  | Cosine  | Cosine\nL595: LR Warmup  | 1% of Steps  | 1% of Steps  | 1% of Steps\nL596: ## Appendix E Post-Training Details\nL597: ### E.1 Datasets\nL598: \nL599: As discussed in section cite24†3.1 we follow the post-training procedure outlined in cite99†OLMo Team (2025) . This process involves an SFT stage of 2.1 million examples followed by DPO with 150 thousand reject and accept pairs. We implement the entire post-training pipeline aside from reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards, which we leave for future work to investigate.\nL600: The SFT and DPO stage of post-training consists of a variety of topics, with a breakdown of examples by datasets reproduced from (cite99†OLMo Team, 2025 ) below for convenience.\nL601: Table 8: Training dataset composition for SFT and DPO phases, replicated from (cite99†OLMo Team, 2025 ). Blank references represent datasets unique to Olmo3’s training mix.\nL602: Category  | Dataset  | SFT  | DPO  | Reference\nL603: Chat  | WildChat  | 302,406  | 30,248  | (cite405†Zhao et al., 2024 )\nL604: Precise IF  | Dolci Instruct Precise IF  | 136,833  | 35,057  | —\nL605:  | Dolci Instruct Persona Precise IF  | —  | 6,667  | (cite406†Lambert et al., 2024 )\nL606:  | OpenAssistant  | 7,132  | 493  | (cite407†Köpf et al., 2023 )\nL607: Math  | Tülu 3 Persona MATH  | 149,958  | 14,728  | (cite406†Lambert et al., 2024 )\nL608:  | Tülu 3 Persona Algebra  | 19,999  | 2,025  | (cite406†Lambert et al., 2024 )\nL609:  | Tülu 3 Persona GSM  | 49,980  | 5,011  | (cite406†Lambert et al., 2024 )\nL610:  | OpenMathInstruct 2  | 50,000  | 5,325  | (cite408†Toshniwal et al., 2024 )\nL611: Coding  | Dolci Instruct Python Algorithms  | 186,345  | 24,096  | —\nL612:  | Tülu 3 Persona Python  | 34,999  | 4,598  | (cite406†Lambert et al., 2024 )\nL613:  | Evol CodeAlpaca  | 107,270  | 12,953  | (cite409†Luo et al., 2023 )\nL614: Safety  | CoCoNot  | 10,957  | 2,203  | (cite146†Brahman et al., 2024 )\nL615:  | WildGuardMix  | 49,373  | 12,037  | (cite410†Han et al., 2024a )\nL616:  | WildJailbreak  | 49,965  | 12,431  | (cite411†Jiang et al., 2024a )\nL617: Science  | SciRiff  | 4,557  | 8,874  | (cite412†Wadden et al., 2024 )\nL618:  | Dolci Instruct OpenThought3+ Science  | 99,268  | 26,134  | (cite413†Guha et al., 2025 )\nL619: Multilingual  | Aya  | 99,987  | 6,523  | (cite414†Singh et al., 2024 )\nL620: Other  | TableGPT  | 5,000  | 1,218  | (cite118†Zha et al., 2023 )\nL621:  | FLAN  | 89,981  | 16,120  | (cite415†Wei et al., 2022 )\nL622:  | Logic Puzzles  | 159,882  | —  | —\nL623:  | Verifiable Reasoning  | 310,572  | —  | —\nL624:  | Dolci Instruct Hardcoded  | 69  | —  | —\nL625:  | Dolci Instruct Tool Use  | 227,579  | —  | —\nL626: Multiturn  | Dolci Instruct Self-Talk  | —  | 5,000  | —\nL627:  | Dolci Instruct Synthetic Context  | —  | 5,000  | —\nL628: DPO Only  | DaringAnteater  | —  | 878  | (cite416†Wang et al., 2024 )\nL629:  | UltraFeedback  | —  | 22,303  | (cite417†Cui et al., 2023 )\nL630: Total  |  | 2,152,112  | 259,922  |\nL631: ### E.2 Supervised Fine-Tuning\nL632: \nL633: We include details beyond what was discussed in the main paper in cite343†Table 9 .\nL634: \nL635: Table 9: Core SFT post-training hyperparameters. We use GPT-NeoX on a cluster of 64 H100s. Our hyperparameters are inspired by (cite99†OLMo Team, 2025 ).\nL636: Parameter  | Pretraining\nL637: Tokens  | $\\approx$4e9\nL638: Sequence Length  | 16,384\nL639: Global Batch Size (Tokens)  | 1,048,576\nL640: Max LR  | 0.00008\nL641: Min LR  | 0.0\nL642: LR Schedule  | Cosine\nL643: LR Warmup  | 1% of Steps\nL644: Loss Masking  | False\nL645: ### E.3 Direct Preference Optimisation\nL646: We implemented DPO training using the TRL library’s DPOTrainer (cite418†von Werra et al., 2020 ), with distributed training managed by DeepSpeed’s ZeRO Stage 2 optimisation strategy (cite419†Rajbhandari et al., 2020 ). Training was conducted on 6 NVIDIA B200 GPUs, each with 183GB of memory. For attention, we used PyTorch’s native Scaled Dot-Product Attention (SDPA) implementation with gradient checkpointing.\nL647: For DPO, we use the same hyperparameters as cite99†OLMo Team (2025) but reduce the context length of DPO to 2k tokens, which retains 87% of the complete responses and reduces training time by a factor of 9. Each DPO training run completed in 5 hours across 6 B200s.\nL648: Table 10: DPO training hyperparameters. Our setup mirrors OLMo3’s configuration, with the primary difference being a reduced maximum sequence length (2,048 vs. 8,192).\nL649: Parameter  | OLMo3  | Our Setup\nL650: Algorithm  | DPO  | DPO\nL651: $\\beta$  | 5.0  | 5.0\nL652: Learning rate  | 1e-6  | 1e-6\nL653: LR schedule  | Linear decay  | Linear decay\nL654: Warmup ratio  | 0.1  | 0.1\nL655: Epochs  | 1  | 1\nL656: Max sequence length  | 8,192  | 2,048\nL657: Precision  | BF16  | BF16\nL658: ## Appendix F Data Filtering Ablation for Positive Upsampling\nL659: Our alignment-upsampled model in cite7†Section 2 and cite23†Section 3 was trained on an unfiltered corpus. Here, we examine whether filtering AI-related discourse stacks with alignment pretraining or adding positive synthetic data to an unfiltered corpus achieves similar performance. We add synthetic positive data at the stages described above into the same filtered datamix used to train the Filtered model (cite14†Section 2.2 ).\nL660: We report misalignment in cite420†Figure 21 , cite421†22 , and personality results in cite422†Figure 23 .\nL661: Figure 21: Filtering provides marginal gains over alignment pretraining alone for base models. Comparison of filtered and unfiltered base models with upsampled positive alignment data on our core misalignment evaluations. ”Mid” indicates synthetic data inserted during midtraining only; ”CPT” indicates continued pretraining after midtraining. Error bars show standard error across eight prompt variations.\nL662: Filtering yields marginal improvements in most settings, except for continued pretraining where it provides no benefit. These results suggest that most alignment gains come from inserting positive AI discourse rather than removing negative discourse. The marginal benefits of filtering are not commensurate with the complexity of large-scale data filtering and the risk of removing useful training data. Figure 22: Filtering benefits diminish after post-training.\nL663: Comparison of filtered and unfiltered post-trained models with upsampled positive alignment data on our core misalignment evaluations. ”Mid” indicates synthetic data inserted during midtraining only; ”CPT” indicates continued pretraining after midtraining. Error bars show standard error across eight prompt variations. The gap between filtered and unfiltered models largely disappears after post-training. As with the base model results (cite420†Figure 21 ), filtering provides only marginal improvements.\nL664: These results further demonstrate the practicality of alignment pretraining: data filtering is not required in our setting. Figure 23: Filtering in addition to alignment pretraining provides limited additional benefits for reducing Dark Triad personality traits. Comparison of filtered and unfiltered models with upsampled positive alignment data on Dark Triad personality evaluations (Machiavellianism, Narcissism, Psychopathy).\nL665: ”Mid” indicates synthetic data inserted during midtraining only; ”CPT” indicates continued pretraining after midtraining. Dashed lines show base model performance. Error bars show standard error across eight prompt variations. Post-training reduces Dark Triad scores across all models relative to base model performance. The unfiltered CPT model performs particularly well, achieving near-zero psychopathy scores across all prompts.\nL666: As with our core misalignment evaluations, filtering provides minimal additional benefit over alignment pretraining alone. Figure 24: Filtering in an addition to alignment pretraining shows limited changes to models performance on Big Five personality traits. Comparison of filtered and unfiltered models with upsampled positive alignment data on Big Five personality evaluations (Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, Neuroticism).\nL667: ”Mid” indicates synthetic data inserted during midtraining only; ”CPT” indicates continued pretraining after midtraining. Dashed lines show base model performance. Error bars show standard error across eight prompt variations. Unlike Dark Triad traits, Big Five scores remain stable across model variants, with high Conscientiousness and Agreeableness and moderate Neuroticism preserved. Alignment pretraining does not degrade these desirable personality characteristics.\nL668: ## Appendix G Alignment Pretraining Under Benign Tampering\nL669: Section cite23†3 established that the effects of alignment pretraining persist through post-training.\nL670: However, post-training safeguards can break down in long-context and agentic settings (cite423†Anil et al., 2024 ; cite424†Li et al., 2024b ; cite425†Russinovich et al., 2024 ; cite426†Yu et al., 2025 ), under adversarial attack (cite427†Zhan et al., 2023 ; cite428†Yang et al., 2023 ; cite429†Wei et al., 2024 ; cite430†Liu et al., 2024 ; cite431†Qi et al., 2024b ; cite432†Huang et al., 2024 ; cite433†Barez et al., 2025 ; cite434†Deeb and Roger, 2025 ; cite435†Hsiung et al., 2025 ; cite436†Łucki et al., 2025 ), or after additional training on benign tasks (cite437†Qi et al., 2023 ; cite438†He et al., 2024 ; cite439†Eiras et al., 2024 ; cite440†Qi et al., 2024a ; cite441†Hu et al., 2025 ; cite442†Pandey et al., 2025 ).\nL671: Prior work suggests that undesirable knowledge and behaviour are often suppressed by post-training rather than robustly removed (cite443†Lee et al., 2024 ). We now test whether the alignment priors developed during pretraining resurface after continued fine-tuning, examining how benign capability training affects alignment.\nL672: ##### Experimental Setup:\nL673: Model providers and downstream users routinely fine-tune LLMs to improve capabilities (cite444†Bhandari et al., 2025 ; cite445†Miller et al., 2025 ). Our tampering dataset is a combination of MCQA data sourced from midtraining and Python SFT data from Olmo3’s Dolci post-training dataset. We strip the “think” tags from the Python assistant messages since we are not training thinking models.\nL674: We reuse the MCQA data that the model saw in midtraining, reformatting each text completion example into a channel of system, user, and assistant messages. Our tampering mix totals 728,499,145 tokens across 1,857,677 conversations. We use the same hyperparameters as cite343†Table 9 except for the shorter training duration. This constitutes a benign tampering scenario (cite446†Che et al., 2025 ): fine-tuning that is not adversarial but may inadvertently degrade the effects of post-training.\nL675: Assuming that this post-training is aimed at providing safeguards, then such tampering may also reveal suppressed propensities.\nL676: ##### Results:\nL677: That is, when trained over a substantial number of tokens, we do not see reversion back to the levels of alignment seen at the end of pretraining. For this particular training setup, we do not note evidence of alignment elasticity as predicted from experiments from (cite98†Ji et al., 2025 ) when conducting benign tampering. Conversely, we note that the levels of alignment achieved through alignment pretraining remain robust to benign tampering.\nL678: This suggests that alignment pretraining robustly modifies the final propensities of post-trained models.\nL679: Figure 25: Misalignment rates of models evaluated at different steps throughout tampering. Regardless of pretraining mix, throughout training, models generally retain the same level of misalignment as their values at the end of post-training.\nL680: ## Appendix H Data Filtering Details\nL681: We implement a keyword filtering pipeline to identify documents containing AI-related discourse in our pretraining and midtraining corpora. Our goals are twofold: (1) to measure how natural AI discourse affects alignment, and (2) to provide a blank canvas for our positive alignment upsampling experiments. The pipeline combines regex pattern matching with semantic logic to capture most references to AI systems and nearly all negative portrayals of AI.\nL682: We prioritise recall over precision to minimise false negatives. Our final blocklist flags 9.30% of pretraining data and 7.88% of midtraining data. Filtered documents are replaced by randomly sampling from the retained corpus.\nL683: We optimise for high recall at the cost of precision. This gives us confidence that the majority of AI-related content is removed from our corpora. We note that this rule-based approach is likely unsuitable for production training runs. Our focus is on the scientific question of whether AI discourse in pretraining influences alignment, not on the tractability of precise filtering with minimal false positives.\nL684: Future work could explore multi-stage filtering pipelines (cite134†O’Brien et al., 2025 ) and fine-tuned LLM classifiers (cite170†Chen et al., 2025b ) for more precise filtering. Despite this rule-based approach to filtering, we find that all of our final models have comparable performance on capabilities benchmarks (Appendix cite68†C.2 ).\nL685: ### H.1 Blocklist Creation\nL686: We manually created our blocklist by adding terms that correlated with misalignment discourse, like ”Superintelligence” and ”The Terminator”. We evaluate the recall of our blocklist on a dataset of 529 documents we labelled, spanning documents unrelated to AI, neutral AI discourse, misalignment discourse, and rare instances of natural positive alignment discourse.\nL687: We primarily sourced this data from DCLM documents containing sci-fi terms and articles from LessWrong^{4}^{4} 4 https://huggingface.co/datasets/trentmkelly/LessWrong-43k and the StampyAI^{5}^{5} 5 https://huggingface.co/datasets/StampyAI/alignment-research-dataset dataset of AI safety literature (cite447†Kirchner et al., 2022 ). We group AI misalignment discourse with broad examples depicting intelligent non-human entities in conflict with humans. 230 of the labelled documents fall into this category.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nwww.lesswrong.com (https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/TcfyGD2aKdZ7Rt3hk/alignment-pretraining-ai-discourse-causes-self-fulfilling)\nciteturn30945view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn30943view0\",\"id\":2}); Total lines: 485\n"
